  "worked_solution": "Let $h$ be the length of the diagonals and $A$ be the area of the square. Using the Pythagorean theorem, the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2.2\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-2.2.\n$$\n\nWhen the diagonal length is $h=10,$ substituting into the expression above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=(-2.2)(10)=-22{\\text{\u00a0mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square opening is decreasing at a rate of $22{\\text{\u00a0mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$",
